From: Loong <1031313955@qq.com>
Subject: [PATCH] xg-040g-md: name the web recovery build in the bootmenu

The board boots a U-Boot that serves its own recovery page, but nothing on
screen says so.  The menu is indistinguishable from a stock UBI build, and a
user who ends up in it has no way back to the project that produced it.

  - the banner carries the recovery page's version, so a bug report names the
    build it came from.  The three pairs of parentheses around it are gone:
    the title is drawn from column 3 and _bootmenu_update_title appends the
    full $ver, so those twelve columns were the difference between seeing
    the revision and hash or having them cut off;

  - entry 9 starts httpd by hand.  Until now the server was only reachable by
    holding reset at power-on, or by failing to boot from NAND -- neither is
    available to someone already sitting at the menu.  Drawn red like the two
    entries that write the bootloader: it is a flashing entry, and it takes
    the board off the menu until it is aborted;

  - entry 10 carries the project page.  U-Boot's bootmenu only has single
    character shortcuts (1-9, then a-z, 0 reserved for Exit), so it is drawn
    as "a."  The URL sits in the title rather than behind the keypress: the
    point is to be readable without pressing anything.  Selecting it prints
    the guide and author pages as well;

  - httpd prints the same on the console, for the serial user who never sees
    the page, and prints the MAC while it is at it.  net_check_prereq() only
    validates the MAC for BOOTP/DHCP/LINKLOCAL, not for HTTPD, so a board
    that came up with a random or empty ethaddr still announces "Listening
    for HTTP" and still answers nothing -- the browser is talking to whatever
    ARP learned last time.  That failure is invisible without this line.

ethaddr_factory is reworked while we are here.  It used to write whatever it
read at ri+0x3e into ethaddr unconditionally, so a board whose ri volume was
recreated empty -- anything that went through "rebuild UBI" -- came up with
ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, an erased-NAND page read as a MAC.  eth-uclass.c only tests
the environment MAC for is_zero_ethaddr(), never is_valid_ethaddr(), so the
broadcast address sails straight through and is used as a source address: DHCP
still works (it is broadcast anyway) while every unicast reply is dropped by
the peer, which is exactly as confusing as it sounds.

It now reads into a scratch variable and only assigns when what came back is
usable.  That variable is named rimac rather than anything starting with an
underscore: hush only begins a variable name on an alpha character
(cli_hush.c, "if (isalpha(ch))"), so $_mac would have been passed through
literally -- and then rejected by the MAC type check on ethaddr, which is how
this was found.  The existing _firstboot and friends are fine because they
only ever appear as arguments to run and setenv, never expanded.  Deciding on the value read rather than on whether ethaddr is already
set is the part that matters: _firstboot runs from the bootmenu, which is well
after initr_net(), and a board with no MAC has by then been given a generated
one that eth-uclass wrote into the environment.  A guard of the "skip if
ethaddr exists" kind therefore always fires, and the real address in ri would
never be read back after a factory reset.  Keying on the value instead means a
populated ri wins over the generated address, while an erased one leaves it
alone -- which is also what protects a MAC set by hand.

The version appears twice, in bootmenu_title and in WEB_VERSION: the env file
is plain text and cannot see the macro.  Both live in this patch so that they
move together.

A saved environment shadows the one compiled in, so none of this would reach
a board that has already been set up: ubootenv holds a complete copy, and a
new FIP cannot get a word in.  Telling people to "Reset all settings to
factory defaults" works but throws out ethaddr with it, which is exactly the
failure above.

So the default environment gains an envver, and an EVT_POST_PREBOOT spy
re-imports just the menu variables when the saved copy is behind -- after
preboot has run, before bootdelay_process() and autoboot_command() draw the
menu.  The list deliberately excludes everything the running system owns:
bootdelay and bootmenu_delay (raised 0 -> 3 by _switch_to_menu), bootmenu_0
(swapped for bootmenu_0d on init), and ethaddr, which is not in the default
environment at all and therefore cannot be clobbered by an import.

The same spy file also drops an ethaddr of ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff at
EVT_SETTINGS_R -- after initr_env(), before initr_net(), the one window where
it still changes what the NIC comes up with.  The env-side guard added
alongside only runs from _firstboot, so a board that already had the bad
value saved would never have met it.

Doing it here rather than at flash time is what makes it exact: "env default"
imports whatever is compiled into the *running* U-Boot, so refreshing while
flashing a new FIP would install the outgoing defaults and leave the menu one
version behind forever.  The new U-Boot has to be the one that does it.

Re-importing bootmenu_title also means putting $ver back by hand.  The env
script that normally appends it, _bootmenu_update_title, deletes itself the
first time it runs -- it is there for the one-time setup of a fresh
environment -- so after a refresh nothing would be left to do it and the menu
would advertise no version at all.  The two never double up: a rebuilt
environment runs the script and already matches envver, while an upgrade
fires the spy long after the script is gone.

The spy lands in the shared an7581 board file, which is fine: the other
boards have no envver in their default environment and return early.  Saving
is best-effort -- a first-time migration gets here before _init_env has made
the env volumes, and is running off the defaults anyway.

--- a/board/airoha/an7581/an7581_rfb.c
+++ b/board/airoha/an7581/an7581_rfb.c
@@ -4,6 +4,12 @@
  */
 
 #include <asm/global_data.h>
+#include <env.h>
+#include <event.h>
+#include <search.h>
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
 
 DECLARE_GLOBAL_DATA_PTR;
 
@@ -14,3 +20,129 @@ int board_init(void)
 
 	return 0;
 }
+
+/*
+ * A new FIP ships a new default environment, but the saved one shadows it
+ * completely: ubootenv holds a full copy, so menu entries added since the
+ * board was first initialised never appear, and the title goes on advertising
+ * the version it was installed with.
+ *
+ * The blunt answer -- "Reset all settings to factory defaults" -- also clears
+ * ethaddr, and on a board whose ri volume was rebuilt that is the only copy of
+ * the MAC left.  So instead the default environment carries an envver, and
+ * this re-imports just the variables that spell out the menu whenever the
+ * saved copy is behind.
+ *
+ * Everything the running system owns is deliberately left out of that list:
+ * bootdelay and bootmenu_delay (raised from 0 to 3 by _switch_to_menu),
+ * bootmenu_0 (swapped for bootmenu_0d once the board is initialised), and
+ * ethaddr, which is not in the default environment at all.
+ *
+ * A board that was never initialised has no saved environment to be behind:
+ * env_get() is already answering out of the compiled-in default, so the
+ * versions match and nothing happens.  Other an7581 boards have no envver in
+ * their default environment and return early.
+ */
+/*
+ * ethaddr_factory reads six bytes out of the ri volume.  On a board that went
+ * through "rebuild UBI" that volume was recreated empty, so what it reads is
+ * an erased NAND page and what lands in ethaddr is ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff.
+ *
+ * eth-uclass only tests the environment MAC with is_zero_ethaddr(), never
+ * is_valid_ethaddr(), so the broadcast address is taken at face value and
+ * then used as a source address -- DHCP still works, being broadcast anyway,
+ * while every unicast reply is dropped by the peer.  The env script now
+ * guards against this, but it only runs from _firstboot, so a board already
+ * carrying the bad value in its saved environment never meets the fix.
+ *
+ * EVT_SETTINGS_R fires after initr_env() and before initr_net(), which is the
+ * one window where clearing it still changes what the NIC comes up with.
+ */
+static int ethaddr_dropped;
+
+static int drop_broken_ethaddr(void)
+{
+	const char *mac = env_get("ethaddr");
+
+	if (!mac || strcmp(mac, "ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff"))
+		return 0;
+
+	printf("ethaddr is ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff -- an erased ri volume read as a MAC.\n");
+	printf("Dropping it; a generated address will be used and saved.\n");
+	printf("To put the real one back: setenv ethaddr <MAC on the label> ; saveenv\n");
+
+	env_set("ethaddr", NULL);
+	ethaddr_dropped = 1;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+EVENT_SPY_SIMPLE(EVT_SETTINGS_R, drop_broken_ethaddr);
+
+static char * const menu_vars[] = {
+	"envver",
+	"bootmenu_title",
+	"bootmenu_1", "bootmenu_2", "bootmenu_3", "bootmenu_4", "bootmenu_5",
+	"bootmenu_6", "bootmenu_7", "bootmenu_8", "bootmenu_9",
+	"show_about",
+};
+
+/*
+ * _bootmenu_update_title is what appends $ver to the title, and it deletes
+ * itself the first time it runs -- it exists for the one-time setup of a
+ * fresh environment.  So after re-importing bootmenu_title below there is
+ * nothing left to put the version back, and the menu would go on showing no
+ * version at all.  Do it here instead.
+ *
+ * The two never double up: a rebuilt environment runs the script and matches
+ * envver (so this does not fire), while a firmware upgrade fires this and the
+ * script is long gone.
+ */
+static void append_version_to_title(void)
+{
+	const char *title = env_get("bootmenu_title");
+	const char *ver = env_get("ver");
+	char buf[256];
+
+	if (!title || !ver)
+		return;
+
+	snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "%s       \033[33m%s\033[0m", title, ver);
+	env_set("bootmenu_title", buf);
+}
+
+static int refresh_boot_menu(void)
+{
+	char want[16];
+	const char *have;
+	int dirty = ethaddr_dropped;
+
+	if (env_get_default_into("envver", want, sizeof(want)) >= 0 && want[0]) {
+		have = env_get("envver");
+		if (!have || strcmp(have, want)) {
+			printf("Boot menu: refreshing saved environment for v%s\n",
+			       want);
+			env_set_default_vars(ARRAY_SIZE(menu_vars), menu_vars,
+					     H_INTERACTIVE);
+			append_version_to_title();
+			dirty = 1;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (!dirty)
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * By now eth-uclass has replaced a dropped ethaddr with a generated
+	 * one; saving is what stops it being a different address on every
+	 * boot, which would leave every peer holding a stale ARP entry.
+	 *
+	 * Saving is best-effort on purpose.  A first-time migration reaches
+	 * here before _init_env has created the env volumes, and it does not
+	 * need the write anyway -- it is already running off the defaults.
+	 */
+	if (env_save())
+		printf("Environment not saved, changes apply to this boot only\n");
+
+	return 0;
+}
+EVENT_SPY_SIMPLE(EVT_POST_PREBOOT, refresh_boot_menu);
--- a/configs/an7581_nokia_xg-040g-md_defconfig
+++ b/configs/an7581_nokia_xg-040g-md_defconfig
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@ CONFIG_SYS_MAXARGS=8
 CONFIG_CMD_CPU=y
 CONFIG_CMD_LICENSE=y
 CONFIG_CMD_BOOTMENU=y
+CONFIG_EVENT=y
 CONFIG_CMD_ASKENV=y
 CONFIG_CMD_ERASEENV=y
 CONFIG_CMD_ENV_FLAGS=y
--- a/defenvs/an7581_nokia_xg-040g-md_env
+++ b/defenvs/an7581_nokia_xg-040g-md_env
@@ -10,10 +10,11 @@ bootfile_bl2=immortalwrt-airoha-an7581-nokia_xg-040g-md-ubi-preloader.bin
 bootfile_fip=immortalwrt-airoha-an7581-nokia_xg-040g-md-ubi-bl31-uboot.fip
 bootfile_upg=immortalwrt-airoha-an7581-nokia_xg-040g-md-ubi-squashfs-sysupgrade.itb
 bootled_status=green:power
+envver=3
 bootmenu_confirm_return=askenv - Press ENTER to return to menu ; bootmenu 60
 bootmenu_default=0
 bootmenu_delay=0
-bootmenu_title=      [0;34m( ( ( [1;39mOpenWrt[0;34m ) ) )
+bootmenu_title=      [1;39mOpenWrt-Web 0.1.2[0m
 bootmenu_0=Initialize environment.=run _firstboot
 bootmenu_0d=Run default boot command.=run boot_default
 bootmenu_1=Boot system via TFTP=run boot_tftp ; run bootmenu_confirm_return
@@ -23,6 +24,8 @@ bootmenu_4=[31mLoad BL31+U-Boot FIP via TFTP then write to NAND[0m=run boot_tf
 bootmenu_5=[31mLoad BL2 preloader via TFTP then write to NAND[0m=run boot_tftp_write_bl2 ; run bootmenu_confirm_return
 bootmenu_6=Reboot=reset
 bootmenu_7=Reset all settings to factory defaults=run reset_factory ; reset
+bootmenu_8=[31mStart web recovery server (http://192.168.1.1)[0m=httpd ; run bootmenu_confirm_return
+bootmenu_9=About - github.com/Loong1996/ImmortalWrt-XG-040G-MD=run show_about ; run bootmenu_confirm_return
 boot_first=if button reset ; then led $bootled_status on ; run boot_default ; fi ; bootmenu
 boot_default=run bootcmd ; run boot_tftp_forever
 boot_production=led $bootled_status on ; run ubi_read_production && bootm $loadaddr#$bootconf ; led $bootled_status off
@@ -36,7 +39,8 @@ boot_tftp_write_bl2=mw.b $loadaddr 0xff 0x800 ; setexpr loadaddr_bl2 $loadaddr +
 boot_tftp_write_fip=tftpboot $loadaddr $bootfile_fip && run ubi_write_fip && run reset_factory
 preboot=led $bootled_status on
 check_buttons=if button reset ; then httpd ; fi
-ethaddr_factory=ubi read 0x90000000 ri && env readmem -b ethaddr 0x9000003e 0x6 ; setenv ethaddr_factory
+show_about=echo ; echo Web recovery U-Boot by Loong ; echo Guide: https://loong1996.github.io/ImmortalWrt-XG-040G-MD/recovery-guide.html ; echo Project: https://github.com/Loong1996/ImmortalWrt-XG-040G-MD ; echo Author: https://github.com/Loong1996 ; echo
+ethaddr_factory=ubi read 0x90000000 ri && env readmem -b rimac 0x9000003e 0x6 ; if test "$rimac" = "ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff" ; then echo ri volume holds no factory MAC ; setenv rimac ; fi ; if test -n "$rimac" ; then setenv ethaddr "$rimac" ; fi ; setenv rimac ; setenv ethaddr_factory
 part_default=production
 reset_factory=ubi part ubi ; mw $loadaddr 0x0 0x800 ; ubi write $loadaddr ubootenv 0x800 ; ubi write $loadaddr ubootenv2 0x800
 mtd_write_bl2=mtd erase bl2 && mtd write bl2 $loadaddr
--- a/net/httpd.c
+++ b/net/httpd.c
@@ -1044,7 +1044,15 @@ void httpd_start_server(void)
 	net_set_udp_handler(httpd_dhcp_rx);
 	httpd_led_start();
 
-	printf("Using %s device\n", eth_get_name());
+	printf("Web recovery " WEB_VERSION " by Loong\n");
+	printf("Guide   https://loong1996.github.io/ImmortalWrt-XG-040G-MD/recovery-guide.html\n");
+	printf("Project https://github.com/Loong1996/ImmortalWrt-XG-040G-MD\n");
+	/*
+	 * The MAC is worth a line of its own: a board that lost its factory
+	 * MAC still serves the page just fine, but every browser on the
+	 * segment is still talking to whatever ARP learned last time.
+	 */
+	printf("Using %s device, MAC %pM\n", eth_get_name(), net_ethaddr);
 	printf("Listening for HTTP on %pI4 port %d\n", &net_ip, HTTPD_PORT);
 	printf("Handing out DHCP leases from %pI4\n", &net_ip);
 	printf("Press Ctrl-C to abort\n");
